; The two ways a multi-rule macro is written wrong, one refusal each.
; Both refusals changed on 2026-08-16, when MAC-LANG-15 made a rule's
; parameters PATTERNS, and the change is the point of this file:
;
;   sameArity   two rules that nothing can tell apart. This used to be
;               stated as "two rules taking the same number of
;               arguments", because arity was the whole selector and a
;               second rule of a repeated arity could never be
;               reached. That reason is now false - three rules of
;               arity one, told apart by shape, are what
;               `tests/selfhost/392-macro-patterns.ax` is - so the
;               test narrowed to what was always the real claim: an
;               IRREFUTABLE rule, every element a plain binder, takes
;               every invocation of its arity and starves anything of
;               that arity after it. Both rules here are all-binder,
;               so both the old rule and the new one refuse, and the
;               file measures that the narrowing did not lose the
;               case it was narrowed from.
;
;               It also became its own code. `AX3020` was chosen when
;               the two conditions could be said to agree ("this macro
;               declares the same thing twice"); a repeated PARAMETER
;               and an unreachable RULE do not agree, and a slug
;               reading `macro-duplicate-parameter` on the second is a
;               machine-readable field that is simply wrong. It is
;               `AX3033`, `macro-unreachable-rule`
;   the call    an invocation matching no rule. The diagnostic used to
;               name every ARITY the macro offers - `takes 1 or 3
;               arguments` - and now names every SHAPE: `(spread T) or
;               (spread T a b)`. An arity list is everything
;               arity-selection could say and says nothing once two
;               rules of one arity differ by shape
;
; Both are at the place the reader can act on: the macro for the
; unreachable rule, the invocation for the shape that matched none.
(data D (A))

(macro sameArity ((sameArity T)
   (:: (syntax/join p T) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join p T)) 1))
  ((sameArity U)
   (:: (syntax/join q U) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join q U)) 2)))

(macro spread ((spread T)
   (:: (syntax/join r T) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join r T)) 1))
  ((spread T a b)
   (:: (syntax/join r T) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join r T)) 3)))

(sameArity D)

(spread D 9)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
